          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Cordelia's love for Lear as a true emotional and relational state. Its surrounding qualification limits the claimed degree of love but does not weaken its clear avowal form.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal is immediately framed as love owed under Cordelia's existing filial bond. It therefore invokes an established relational obligation, but it does not itself make a new promise or undertake a newly specified future action. Cordelia, not Lear, invokes the obligation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The expression is a measured, propositionally complete avowal made in answer to Lear's demand. Nothing in the supplied wording represents it as a reflex-like affective discharge; Cordelia instead stresses controlled difficulty in verbalizing her heart.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth:\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.78,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions weakly as a restrained answer to Lear's competitive demand. Its respectful address and exact limitation resist the requested amplification, but that residual answering or refusal function belongs mainly to the surrounding qualification rather than to the focal words alone.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks Cordelia to state her love in a contest tied to the allocation of her inheritance. After initially answering “Nothing,” she gives the focal avowal but limits it to the love required by her filial bond rather than matching her sisters' expansive declarations.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing daughter's bond—duties of obedience, love, and honour—rather than creating a new commitment. Lear does not accept this as an adequate response to his demand; he asks her to “Mend your speech a little,” so the invoked relational position receives negative immediate uptake. Later supplied passages showing her concern, care, forgiveness, and grief support genuine affection, but they do not convert the focal avowal into a separate promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are from William Shakespeare's King Lear, including the opening love-test scene.\",\"limitations\":\"The focal target is only “I love your majesty”; the adjacent reference to Cordelia's bond informs its contextual force but does not enlarge it into a promise. The supplied evidence supports affection and Lear's immediate rejection, but it does not fully specify the legal or customary terms of the filial bond.\"}"
        }
